WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.840
WILL WANG: Hi, everyone.

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:01.950
Today we're going
to talk about how

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:05.580
to streamline your end-to-end
developer workflow with AI.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.450
My name is Will.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.270
I'm the founder of Clova,
where we build an AI agent

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.520
that allows different brands
and businesses to be able

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.440
to quickly generate
on-brand assets

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.090
and iterate through
them as well.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.530
We're also backed by
the Founders Inc.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.770
Accelerator here in San
Francisco within Fort Mason.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.080
And, in my free time, I
enjoy making YouTube videos

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.790
where I document
my startup journey

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.580
with over 18,000
subscribers on the channel.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.280
And I just share about the ups
and lows of building a startup

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.320
and also share about
how I build software.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.330
And today we're going to
cover the four phases

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.070
of shipping a
product end to end.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.990
In the first phase, we're going
to talk about how to come

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.460
up with startup ideas.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.680
In the second phase, we're going
to talk about how do we build

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.480
with Copilot and VS Code.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.220
In the third phase, we're
going to talk about how

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.710
to effectively communicate
with our team so we're able

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.290
to move fast while
building quality software.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.300
And, lastly, once we
have our software built,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.130
we're going to talk about
how do we go to market

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.660
to get real-life users.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.450
To come up with the idea, the
number one thing I suggest is

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.910
to build for a problem
you have for yourself.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.680
The first reason is
that, if you already run

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.240
into the problem yourself,

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.560
most likely you
already understand it

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.000
on a very deep level.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.520
And the second reason is that,
for a problem you already have

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.040
for yourself, you will also
be using your own software

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.540
to resolve those issues, right?

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.730
So you're able to go through
rapid iteration loops.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.670
The second thing is to be
a domain expert in a field

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.790
that you're trying to
solve the problem for.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.460
Again, that also helps with
understanding the problem.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:23.870
The second thing is
that you're able

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:27.140
to most likely know other
people in the industry already,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.750
so you're able to find
early adopters very quickly

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.410
through your network.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.540
And a lot of times people
think, when you come

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.850
up with a startup idea, you
want to sit down and think

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.800
about what good ideas there is.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.490
But, from my own experiences,
a lot of times all the ideas,

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.720
where they come from is when I'm
not actually actively trying

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.640
to think of different
ideas to build.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.220
For example, let's say if
I'm browsing the internet,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.350
and what happens is
that you always want

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.880
to catch the ideas
the moment they come

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.890
up because they can
very quickly go away.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.420
So something that
has been helpful is

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.540
that I'm using the Logitech
MX Master 4 over here is

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.520
that they actually have
an action ring here.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.700
So over here I configure it over
here to open up a new notepad.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.850
So I have a documentation
where I'm able

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.860
to quickly write out
different ideas.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.810
Another thing that's also
helpful is that a lot

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.390
of my ideas come from when
I'm actually actively reading

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.670
different articles,
consuming new information.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.340
So I also use the screenshot
feature very often here

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.860
to quickly screenshot the
parts, so I can also save

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.500
that into my notes so I
have it for reference.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.960
And, lastly, something that's
also helpful is just to play

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.400
and pause my music, honestly.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.530
Sometimes that puts me in a
different mode when I'm thinking

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.720
of different ideas or also
just when I'm, you know,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.430
working on different
coding projects.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.190
The last method I suggest
is to run user interviews

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.390
about their day to day.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.970
So, for these, I tend
to focus on questions

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.880
like what do you spend the
most time on, what is your day

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.270
to day like, and what does your
organization have budget for?

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.580
Because those things are
very deterministic, right?

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.990
They're not very opinionated.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.030
Since they're just describing
what is happening,

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.380
they won't usually have a bias

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.610
when they're answering
the question.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.470
So, moving on to
the building phase,

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.170
so the three major use cases
I've been using AI for,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.800
number one, will be obviously
writing code with GitHub Copilot

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.870
but also writing a lot
more documentation,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.700
a lot more tests as well.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.550
For the documentation and test
part, I have already tried a lot

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.280
of different prompts and also
iterated a lot to be able

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.190
to consistently get the
results I want, right?

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.530
However, to be able to, you
know, quickly find these prompts

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.300
or copy and paste them in,
I'm definitely not going

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.060
to rewrite them in the
GitHub Copilot Chat.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.310
I actually already
configured my keys

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.740
on the Logitech
Creative MX Keypad.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.020
So each of these keys,
when I'm in VS Code,

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.640
actually is a different prompt.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.290
So I'm able to quickly
paste in these prompts

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.650
and execute these tasks without
having to context-switch and try

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.600
to find where these
prompts are stored

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.730
or rewrite them from scratch.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.420
And something that has also
been very, very helpful is

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.440
that because for each

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.370
of the features I'm shipping I
actually start a new AI session,

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.260
right, because
obviously with AI,

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.180
a limitation is the
context window.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.850
So I don't want to have, like,
a feature that is not relevant

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.890
to the next feature I'm
shipping within there.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.710
But you're able to pass down the
information very effectively.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.110
After I ship a feature,
or after each session,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:08.310
I write down a very detailed AI
doc or also for myself to read

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.650
and for the next AI session
to be able to catch up on

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.720
within that feature, within
that folder as well.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.450
A new thing that has also been
happening with my team is that,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.300
in the past, we're always kind
of debating a lot, right,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.390
about what features to ship,
what experiments to run.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.060
But because of how
much faster we have

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.400
to ship software these
days, instead of debating,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.610
we just try multiple features
and go to production

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:30.960
with it because, at
the end of the day,

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.000
we want to let the
market decide and see

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.380
where the pull is coming from.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.030
So, moving on to Phase
3, we're going to talk

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.300
about how communication has
also really changed with AI

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.460
for different teams
while shipping software.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.160
So, in the past, a lot of times
I would just get on the call

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.650
and explain to my team
what I think is important,

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.240
what the milestones
are trying to hit,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.820
or what other software
issues we're running into.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.910
However, with AI now,
we're able to write

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:56.160
down so much more
documentation at a quick pace.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.660
I actually tell the AI to write

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.540
down a very detailed HTML doc
that's formatted very nicely.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.420
And I present that to my team
because, when I'm on the call

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.030
with them, now, they're able
to really stay aligned more.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:07.950
And I also noticed they're able

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.960
to digest the information
a lot better,

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.880
since there's something visual
for them to follow along with.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.160
And I also obviously share
those documentations with them

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.860
after the call, so they also
have that for reference.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.400
But, also, while showing
documentation on the call,

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.800
I also use Microsoft
Teams for my calls.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.790
So, in Microsoft Teams already,
it takes the transcript;

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:29.030
and obviously the AI also
writes down good documentation

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:31.430
and different action items for
the team to follow as well.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.770
Detailed documentation has
always been super helpful

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.050
for the code base and also on
the business side, as well,

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.910
but they just take
so long to do.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:39.890
And the cool thing is that
a lot of times you're able

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:41.690
to get consistent
behavior from the AI

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:44.060
if you're using the prompt
that you have already tested.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.190
So I actually also
have the prompt saved

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:48.690
on my Logitech MX Creative
Keypad right here.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.060
So I'm going to quickly
access that to write

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:52.590
down these
documentations as well.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:53.610
And, moving on,
we're going to talk

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.530
about once you have your
app shipped, right,

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:56.400
how do you go to market?

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.810
How do you find users?

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.120
So usually what I
suggest is always to go

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.220
where your users are gathering.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.030
And, for different products,
there's different places

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.230
that users gather, right?

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.330
So a lot of the audience
watching right now are

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:10.590
probably developers.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:11.880
So, for the dev community,

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.130
usually what I suggest
will be Twitter.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.200
Also, I suggest Product
Hunt, since a lot of folks

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.360
on Product Hunt are looking
at different products to try.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.610
And a common way that people
have also been doing is

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.410
to create an open-source
project, right,

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.810
since an open-source project,
if you build something cool,

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.350
a lot of times people contribute
to it; and they're very invested

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.510
when they see cool software
that's open source.

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:33.240
And through GitHub and GitHub
Stars, you're able to get a lot

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:34.530
of visibility through that.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.480
For my own personal
software that I'm building

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.610
with Clova right now,
I'm actually doing a lot

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.860
of the marketing on Instagram,

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.360
since a lot of my
users gather there

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.710
and through LinkedIn as well.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:47.190
And something that also has been
helpful from my own experience

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:50.790
and other folks that I see
ship software is also building

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:51.480
in public.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.670
So I do that with YouTube,
right, where I kind

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:54.900
of just document
how I'm building.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.670
And people get invested in
your journey, and they end

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:57.540
up trying your software.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.730
And, once you're able to get
users there, you want to be able

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:02.550
to reach out to them through
email or whatever platform it is

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.710
since you want to get on
these calls with these users

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.440
to get feedback, right, to know
how to build a relationship

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.960
and obviously to
know how to improve

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.370
and iterate on your software.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:14.580
So a lot of times these messages
are also very consistent.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.260
I have a couple messages
already written up.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.480
So I actually already
also have those saved

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.330
on my Logitech MX Creative
Keypad over here as well.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.760
So I'm able to, you know,
simply just change a first name

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.160
and then send out those messages
very, very quickly as well.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.200
But the thing is building
is still the skill.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.110
But the leverage now is that AI
lets us build so much faster,

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.960
so we can put up more shots
and test out way more ideas.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.060
And, after I put it out
there, we can see the signal

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.010
from the market on if
it's an idea we want

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:39.780
to continue working on or not.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.660
But on the flip side, too,
something I have been noticing

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:43.950
for myself and others is

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.380
that a lot more personalized
software has been, you know,

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.050
coming to life, as well,
because in the past it's hard

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.490
to justify the time and energy

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.290
to build those personalized
software, right?

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.720
But now with AI you're able
to bring those ideas to life

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.600
so much quicker, which has
been super, super cool.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.760
And I'm excited to see
what you ship next.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.080
If you do, please let me know.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.100
I'm always excited to check
out new projects as well.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.410
And I hope you learned something
from this presentation,

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.190
and thank you for the time.
